🎯 Attack Logic (The Hacker's Trick)
The Blueprint: The attacker sends a financial-themed phishing email (e.g., an invoice) with a weaponized 
document attached. The document contains an embedded Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) macro design to act 
as a stealthy stager or downloader.

The Mechanism: To prevent security analysts and automated sandboxes from reading the script's true intent, 
the attacker uses string obfuscation techniques (such as string splitting, character arrays, custom XOR, or 
Base64 encoding). When the victim opens the file and allows macros, the script deobfuscates itself in memory, 
reconstructs the malicious commands, and executes them.

Evasion Technique: By ensuring that critical strings (like URLs, file paths, or Windows API functions) do not
appear in cleartext inside the file, the hacker successfully evades static antivirus scanners and traditional 
strings command lookups.

🕵️‍♂️ Investigation Process (How to Spot It)
Safe Triage: Isolate the sample and audit raw text components inside a secure text editor (like Notepad++) or
a dedicated malware analysis sandbox to prevent accidental execution.
Structural Assessment: Run automated OLE audit tools to confirm the presence of concealed VBA macro storage 
streams.
Emulated Deobfuscation: Leverage advanced static analysis engines to simulate code execution, automatically
parsing and uncovering obfuscated layers.
Cryptographic Decoding: Extract the remaining obscured payloads and pass them through processing engines 
(like CyberChef) to reconstruct the final command-and-control (C2) indicators.

